	(text "Own driver: a FET usually dies shorted gate to drain, which would pull a shared gate net to the pack and hold the other bank on. RUN is the only shared signal, and\neach bank takes it through its own diode.\nBoth banks face the same way, drains toward the pack, so regen stays open. Bank B keeps U1, the shunt and the soft start.\nClose order: U8 FLT holds U1 UVLO low through a 100 V diode until BST is up (about 57 ms typ, 35-115 ms with 2.2 uF). The diode keeps a UVLO fault off FLT (20 V\nmax), so A closes first by logic, not by timing. A closes onto MID only. FLT also blocks B if U8 has no charge pump or has tripped.\nOff edge: EN falls first, 4.6 us typ, 6.5 us max, then G1PD pulls 390 nC through 150R: about 10 us, so 6 A/us at 60 A, against a 100 us latch. D1 stays as the\nbackstop. Check at the bench.\nShort-circuit comparator on RS1: 40.2k gives 99 mV, 79-121 mV, 158-242 A, above U1's limit. TMR 100k + 680 pF: about 10 us, latch-off, cleared by an E-stop cycle. It\ncovers reset with B shorted: A opens, and FLT holds B off.\nDriver off holds gate to source with no path to GND, so MID can be biased: 47k + 47k, 0.48-0.50 x pack, 0.6 mA, 63 mW each. Hot FET leakage, up to 200 uA per bank,\nmoves MID by under 5 V. U17 reads MID through 33k (spike and fault limit): calibrate the 3 % gain.\nSelf-test before reset, pack 36-54.6 V, BMS telemetry for pack voltage: MID 0.4-0.6 x pack and U11 VBUS under 3 V is healthy. MID within 2 V of pack: A shorted. MID\nunder 3 V: B shorted. VBUS high: both shorted, or the bus has not bled down yet, about 11 s on board caps, longer with drives.\nU17 uses VBUS only, IN+ and IN- on GND. VBUS alone cannot see one shorted bank, which is why MID is read."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
